  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe goal is to minimize the objective function (lower-is-better score) defined as:\n    score = 2 * n * max(convolve(a)) / (sum(a))^2\nwhere n is the sequence length, a is the sequence of non-negative heights, convolve(a) is the full linear convolution of a with itself, and sum(a) must be at least 0.01.\n\nWe propose a multi-stage optimization approach inspired by wavelet principles. Starting from a seed sequence, we will explore sequences of increasing resolution (lengths 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048) while gradually refining the search strategy. Instead of fixed hyperparameter tuning across all stages, we will systematically optimize parameters such as the decay factor in exponential smoothing, momentum terms, and initial profile configurations.\n\nThe core idea is to use long-short-term memory optimization (a smoothed Adam variant) as the workhorse algorithm, combined with several restart phases that enhance diversity and prevent local minima. By parameterizing the optimization strategy itself, we enable dynamic adaptation to different problem sizes and convexity levels.\n\nKey differences from the provided LP-based method:\n- No explicit linear programming steps in the search; uses gradient-based methods\n- Dynamic parameterization of the optimization process\n- Coherent phase transitions in training sample generation\n- Multiple adaptsign variants tested in sequence\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe objective function remains unchanged:\n    score = (2*n*c_max) / (sum(a))\u00b2  \nwhere c_max is the maximum value of convolve(a, mode='full').\n\nThe search algorithm:\n- Represents the sequence as unnormalized non-negative float array\n- Uses smooth maximum penalization to handle max operation\n- Projects to simplex space for gradient descent\n- Includes exponential smoothing for escape\n- Normalizes by original sum if necessary\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization phase (n1=128):\n   - Starts with height_sequence_1 or other basic profiles\n   - Ensures valid normalization (sum >= 0.01) and non-negativity\n   - Initial objective score computed\n\nb. Layered escape phases (n2=256, n3=512, n4=1024, n5=2048):\n   - Doubling of sequence length to probe higher resolutions\n   - Each phase:\n     - Load best candidate from previous phase\n     - Double its length using straight-line interpolation (divswim)\n     - Compute new objective score\n     - Fall back to previous phase's candidate if target not met\n     - Maintain history of best improvement factors\n\nc. Optimized polish phase (final phase):\n   - Uses logit gradient descent with adaptive learning rates\n   - Runs for 1650 seconds maximum\n   - Computes gradients using frequency-domain analysis\n   - Maintains stable simplex rounding\n   - Checks objective with evaluate_sequence at least every 1000 steps\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nThe optimization process incorporates:\n- Linear, cosine, sine_sq profiles with programmable parameters\n- Random initialization with controlled variance\n- Stage-specific smoothing factors (0.8 to 0.2)\n- Exponential decay parameterization (\u03b2=0.2 to 0.005)\n- Momentum optimization (up=0.9, down=0.999)\n- Manual \u03c4 parameter initialization (periodic restarts)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Each phase carefully manages time:\n  - Initial: < 400 seconds\n  - Growth escape: 1600 seconds total\n  - Final polish: 1650 seconds maximum\n- Uses exponential smoothing starting from 0.25 to handle extreme inversalidity\n- Implements stable simplex rounding to prevent divergence\n- Uses \u03c4=Doubling rate with stringent validation\n- Fall back: preserves best candidate from previous phase\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Independent objective evaluation using evaluate_sequence\n- Final score computed after each escape phase\n- Best score updated only on verified exact calculation\n- Fall back to previous phase if evaluate_sequence fails\n- Returns non-negative sequence with sum at least 0.01\n- Automated comparison against previous best result\n- Maintains strict parameter boundaries (no >1000)\n\nImplementation ensures smooth iterative improvement while respecting evaluation rigour and allowing for resolution jumps. The method explores and leverages multiple parameterized optima simultaneously to enhance the final score.",
